content::
Can we change the scope of the overridden method in the subclass?
Yes, a subclass can increase the visibility of an overridden method, but it cannot reduce it. 
Java does not allow an overridden method to have a more restrictive access modifier than the method in the parent class.
This rule ensures that the overridden method remains accessible wherever the parent method was accessible.
example.
Parent method. public. Child. public. cannot use protected, private.
Parent method. protected. Child. public.protected. cannot use private.
